1
00:00:01,697 --> 00:00:02,698
[keys jangle]

2
00:00:18,931 --> 00:00:22,631
♪ Oh, Danny boy ♪

3
00:00:24,459 --> 00:00:26,026
♪ The pipes ♪

4
00:00:26,113 --> 00:00:32,945
♪ The pipes are calling ♪

5
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:38,038
♪ From Glen to Glen ♪

6
00:00:38,125 --> 00:00:44,392
♪ And down the mountainside ♪

7
00:00:44,479 --> 00:00:50,093
♪ The summer's gone ♪

8
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:56,056
♪ And all the roses falling ♪

9
00:00:58,188 --> 00:01:00,190
♪ 'Tis you ♪

10
00:01:00,277 --> 00:01:04,412
♪ 'Tis you must go ♪

11
00:01:04,499 --> 00:01:09,156
♪ And I must bide ♪♪

12
00:01:12,115 --> 00:01:13,421
[male #1]
'Thank you.'

13
00:01:25,041 --> 00:01:26,390
Which one's ours?

14
00:01:26,477 --> 00:01:28,088
The one who looks like
she blows her nose

15
00:01:28,175 --> 00:01:30,438
with $100 bills.

16
00:01:30,525 --> 00:01:31,700
They all look like that.

17
00:01:31,787 --> 00:01:34,659
Joke. That's her over there.
Let's go.

18
00:01:36,487 --> 00:01:38,272
We shouldn't have
to be asking for charity.

19
00:01:38,359 --> 00:01:41,231
The department needs
the money. Smile.

20
00:01:41,318 --> 00:01:44,147
[ominous music]

21
00:01:59,206 --> 00:02:00,642
[crackling]

22
00:02:03,210 --> 00:02:05,212
Mrs. Doverton. Hi.

23
00:02:05,299 --> 00:02:07,127
I'm Teresa Lisbon.
This is Kimball Cho.

24
00:02:10,869 --> 00:02:12,219
We're with the CBI.

25
00:02:12,306 --> 00:02:14,612
You're sponsoring us
for the event, I believe.

26
00:02:14,699 --> 00:02:17,963
Oh, yes, of course. Yes.
Well, welcome.

27
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:20,792
I was very sorry to hear
about your terrible loss.

28
00:02:20,879 --> 00:02:22,751
Umm, you know,
those agents.

29
00:02:22,838 --> 00:02:24,318
Thank you.

30
00:02:24,405 --> 00:02:26,929
I can only imagine what
you must all be feeling.

31
00:02:27,016 --> 00:02:30,150
We don't talk about it.

32
00:02:30,237 --> 00:02:31,455
Thanks.

33
00:02:35,111 --> 00:02:36,243
[beeps]

34
00:02:37,635 --> 00:02:42,640
[rapid beeping]

35
00:02:42,727 --> 00:02:44,294
[cranking]

36
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:46,905
Well, I'd like to
introduce you to my nephews.

37
00:02:46,992 --> 00:02:49,952
- Uh, this is Thomas.
- Hi.

38
00:02:50,039 --> 00:02:52,041
Say hello, Thomas.

39
00:02:52,128 --> 00:02:53,738
- Hi.
- Hi.

40
00:02:53,825 --> 00:02:56,959
Please call me Tom.

41
00:02:57,046 --> 00:03:01,268
- And this is George.
- So, Miss Lisbon..

42
00:03:01,355 --> 00:03:03,444
What entertainment do you have
planned for us this evening?

43
00:03:03,531 --> 00:03:06,186
Actually, a member of our team
used to be in show business.

44
00:03:06,273 --> 00:03:08,013
He's sort of a magician

45
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:12,192
and he's going to entertain us,
hopefully, yeah.

46
00:03:12,279 --> 00:03:14,890
[indistinct conversations]

47
00:03:14,977 --> 00:03:16,805
How nice.

48
00:03:16,892 --> 00:03:18,372
Cheers.

49
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:27,685
[heavy breathing]

50
00:03:40,437 --> 00:03:42,047
[indistinct chattering]

51
00:03:46,095 --> 00:03:47,488
What?

52
00:03:47,575 --> 00:03:48,489
Just a smile.

53
00:03:50,186 --> 00:03:52,014
He just smiled.

54
00:03:52,101 --> 00:03:55,235
[male #2]
'Ladies and gentleman. Please
welcome Mr. Patrick Jane.'

55
00:03:55,322 --> 00:03:57,367
'of the California Bureau
of Investigation.'

56
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,028
Good evening.

57
00:04:06,115 --> 00:04:07,812
You're a very
good looking crowd.

58
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:10,554
Thank you for coming this
evening. You too, back there.

59
00:04:10,641 --> 00:04:12,817
'You look very regal
up there in the window.'

60
00:04:12,904 --> 00:04:15,255
My name is Patrick Jane.
I'm from the CBI.

61
00:04:15,342 --> 00:04:17,126
Uh, this is an egg.

62
00:04:17,213 --> 00:04:21,086
Yes. I'm supposed to do magic
tricks and read minds for you

63
00:04:21,173 --> 00:04:24,002
but, after some thought,
I, uh..

64
00:04:24,089 --> 00:04:25,700
decided, what the hell.

65
00:04:25,787 --> 00:04:29,791
I don't really want to do
that corny old stuff, so..

66
00:04:29,878 --> 00:04:34,404
[crowd cheers]

67
00:04:34,491 --> 00:04:36,319
Well..

68
00:04:39,714 --> 00:04:43,500
Instead what I'd like
to do is much cooler.

69
00:04:43,587 --> 00:04:47,417
I would like to transform
your consciousness.

70
00:04:48,723 --> 00:04:50,072
Have any of you ever stopped

71
00:04:50,159 --> 00:04:54,250
to consider what lucky bastards
you all are.

72
00:04:54,337 --> 00:04:58,123
If you think the suffering
and the injustice in the world.

73
00:04:58,210 --> 00:05:03,215
Every second of every day
there is someone, somewhere

74
00:05:03,303 --> 00:05:06,523
'crying out in pain and fear.'

75
00:05:08,351 --> 00:05:11,833
'A woman is raped.
A child starves to death.'

76
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:16,359
'A man is executed
for a crime he didn't commit.'

77
00:05:16,446 --> 00:05:18,666
'But, here we are.'

78
00:05:18,753 --> 00:05:22,409
'We could be standing on a roof
in some Bangladeshi village'

79
00:05:22,496 --> 00:05:24,759
'waiting for
the flood waters to recede.'

80
00:05:24,846 --> 00:05:26,021
No!

81
00:05:27,849 --> 00:05:30,808
[gunshots]

82
00:05:30,895 --> 00:05:32,680
But no, here we are

83
00:05:32,767 --> 00:05:36,379
in this lavish ballroom..

84
00:05:36,466 --> 00:05:38,686
...sipping
French champagne and..

85
00:05:38,773 --> 00:05:40,078
declining
the hors d'oeuvres.

86
00:05:40,165 --> 00:05:42,994
Thank you very much. Yes.

87
00:05:43,081 --> 00:05:45,388
Put your hands in the air

88
00:05:45,475 --> 00:05:47,129
anyone that feels lucky.

89
00:05:47,216 --> 00:05:50,001
If you think you are lucky,
put your hands up.

90
00:05:50,088 --> 00:05:51,307
That's right.

91
00:05:51,394 --> 00:05:54,832
We here are all blessed
by fortune.

92
00:05:54,919 --> 00:05:57,966
'If you disagree,
put down your hands.'

93
00:05:58,053 --> 00:06:01,186
'If you don't think
you need to express your thanks'

94
00:06:01,273 --> 00:06:04,189
for that blessing,
put down your hand.

95
00:06:04,276 --> 00:06:06,844
[indistinct conversations]

96
00:06:06,931 --> 00:06:08,542
'If you're not willing
to write a check'

97
00:06:08,629 --> 00:06:12,546
that will save actual lives,
then please put down your hand.

98
00:06:14,548 --> 00:06:15,853
That's great.

99
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:18,856
Very good.

100
00:06:18,943 --> 00:06:21,903
Okay. Now, magic.

101
00:06:21,990 --> 00:06:24,427
You sir, put your left hand
in your jacket pocket

102
00:06:24,514 --> 00:06:26,211
and take out
whatever is in there

103
00:06:26,298 --> 00:06:28,388
and show it to everybody.

104
00:06:31,391 --> 00:06:33,523
[chirping]

105
00:06:33,610 --> 00:06:36,134
'Thank you. Thank you.'

106
00:06:44,273 --> 00:06:46,406
[applause continues]

107
00:06:51,585 --> 00:06:53,587
Make those checks legible.

108
00:06:53,674 --> 00:06:55,545
A novel way of raising money,
I must say.

109
00:06:55,632 --> 00:06:58,418
Ah, well, the direct approach
is sometimes the best.

110
00:06:58,505 --> 00:07:01,377
Oh, do excuse me.

111
00:07:01,464 --> 00:07:03,684
You son of a bitch.
You scared the crap out of me.

112
00:07:03,771 --> 00:07:05,076
Oh, please, like I don't know

113
00:07:05,163 --> 00:07:07,296
how to work
a fat crowd of suckers.

114
00:07:07,383 --> 00:07:10,473
Why, yes.

115
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:12,083
What?

116
00:07:12,170 --> 00:07:13,998
No.

117
00:07:14,085 --> 00:07:15,826
Oh, my god.

118
00:07:15,913 --> 00:07:18,350
What is it?
What's-what's the matter?

119
00:07:18,438 --> 00:07:20,483
Carl's been shot.

120
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:25,401
[theme music]

121
00:07:33,888 --> 00:07:36,281
[police siren blaring]

122
00:07:36,368 --> 00:07:38,327
[Teresa]
'Whoever it was,
they were thorough.'

123
00:07:38,414 --> 00:07:40,329
They bypassed
the alarm system somehow.

124
00:07:40,416 --> 00:07:43,245
Shut off the security cameras.
Prints are unlikely.

125
00:07:43,332 --> 00:07:45,682
'When was the last time
you changed the lock code?'

126
00:07:45,769 --> 00:07:47,684
That was a class five
B vault door.

127
00:07:47,771 --> 00:07:49,643
And I was told that no one
could get through it.

128
00:07:49,730 --> 00:07:51,514
Insurance had damn well
better pay.

129
00:07:51,601 --> 00:07:53,211
A good safecracker
can get through anything

130
00:07:53,298 --> 00:07:55,257
with the right tools
and enough time.

131
00:07:55,344 --> 00:07:56,954
This is beautiful.
Look at that.

132
00:07:57,041 --> 00:08:01,089
Excuse me.
Would you stop that, please?

133
00:08:01,176 --> 00:08:02,525
Sure. No problem.

134
00:08:02,612 --> 00:08:06,616
Just havin' a look.
Got some cool stuff in here.

135
00:08:06,703 --> 00:08:08,792
Very cool.

136
00:08:08,879 --> 00:08:10,141
You two are brothers, right?

137
00:08:10,228 --> 00:08:11,882
Indeed.

138
00:08:11,969 --> 00:08:14,102
It's nice to see that the case
is in such perceptive hands.

139
00:08:14,189 --> 00:08:17,801
Can hands be perceptive?
I don't think so.

140
00:08:17,888 --> 00:08:21,196
So is it usual that your uncle
is here after closing?

141
00:08:21,283 --> 00:08:23,328
He's not our uncle exactly.

142
00:08:23,415 --> 00:08:28,159
Oh, I'm sorry. Uh, the man
having sex with your aunt, then.

143
00:08:32,207 --> 00:08:33,730
No.

144
00:08:33,817 --> 00:08:35,166
No, but tonight was the benefit.

145
00:08:35,253 --> 00:08:38,126
Carl must have forgotten
something and come back

146
00:08:38,213 --> 00:08:39,823
and surprised the thieves.

147
00:08:39,910 --> 00:08:40,998
Hmm. Sure did.

148
00:08:44,436 --> 00:08:46,569
[Patrick]
'What's the deal with Carl
and your aunt?'

149
00:08:46,656 --> 00:08:48,223
'How did they get together?'

150
00:08:48,310 --> 00:08:50,007
They met, uh,
four years ago at the club.

151
00:08:50,094 --> 00:08:52,314
The labor day
senior tennis tournament.

152
00:08:52,401 --> 00:08:54,708
He was a pro back east.

153
00:08:54,795 --> 00:08:57,362
And they married and he joined
the family business?

154
00:08:57,449 --> 00:08:58,929
Yes, that's correct.

155
00:08:59,016 --> 00:09:02,411
Does your resentment of him,
uh, create any tension?

156
00:09:02,498 --> 00:09:03,804
What resentment?

157
00:09:03,891 --> 00:09:06,110
Well, the old girl gets laid.

158
00:09:06,197 --> 00:09:09,592
Next thing you know,
you got a new business partner.

159
00:09:09,679 --> 00:09:11,289
Come on. You resent him.

160
00:09:11,376 --> 00:09:12,508
No.

161
00:09:12,595 --> 00:09:14,641
Carl's been a godsend,
actually.

162
00:09:14,728 --> 00:09:17,252
A natural salesman,
and he's made Esther very happy.

163
00:09:17,339 --> 00:09:19,602
Yes, that's-that's
the main thing.

164
00:09:19,689 --> 00:09:23,127
If I may say so,
what I do very much resent

165
00:09:23,214 --> 00:09:24,433
is the tone
of your questioning.

166
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:26,304
I'm sorry
if we've offended you.

167
00:09:26,391 --> 00:09:28,393
No, don't say that.
This is a murder investigation.

168
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:30,700
I can be as hardass
as I'd like to be. Thank you.

169
00:09:30,787 --> 00:09:33,268
Actually, it's
not a murder investigation yet.

170
00:09:33,355 --> 00:09:35,662
If Mr. Ward dies,
which seems likely

171
00:09:35,749 --> 00:09:37,272
it'll become
a murder investigation.

172
00:09:37,359 --> 00:09:38,795
- Oh. Yeah, well, that's right.
- Yeah.

173
00:09:38,882 --> 00:09:40,275
He's not dead.

174
00:09:40,362 --> 00:09:43,844
Uh, well, uh, I apologize
for my rudeness.

175
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:48,283
Let's go.

176
00:09:48,370 --> 00:09:49,327
Okay.

177
00:09:57,161 --> 00:09:59,337
[female #1]
'We got a break
in the case, Boss.'

178
00:09:59,424 --> 00:10:00,861
This is last night's footage

179
00:10:00,948 --> 00:10:02,950
from a parking lot camera
across the street.

180
00:10:03,037 --> 00:10:06,170
That's Doverton & Co.
Back there on the right.

181
00:10:06,257 --> 00:10:09,783
Okay, so now we zoom in
like this and..

182
00:10:09,870 --> 00:10:12,002
...there. That's him.

183
00:10:12,089 --> 00:10:14,048
Then I checked
the new intersection cameras

184
00:10:14,135 --> 00:10:16,441
the city put in to catch
traffic light violations.

185
00:10:16,528 --> 00:10:18,530
I figured I'd look at the ones
nearest to Doverton's

186
00:10:18,618 --> 00:10:21,098
around that time of night.

187
00:10:21,185 --> 00:10:23,057
Car's registered
to Doyle Murphy.

188
00:10:23,144 --> 00:10:24,362
His sheet's a mile long

189
00:10:24,449 --> 00:10:26,713
and safecracking was
his specialty.

190
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:28,366
I got a current address
in Locke.

191
00:10:28,453 --> 00:10:30,934
Nice work, Van pelt. Quick.

192
00:10:31,021 --> 00:10:33,633
It's your hook. Contact local
PD. And have them pick him up.

193
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:35,243
- Take Rigsby with you.
- Thanks, boss.

194
00:10:35,330 --> 00:10:37,375
It's all very neat and easy.

195
00:10:37,462 --> 00:10:39,464
Hey, sometimes it happens
that way.

196
00:10:39,551 --> 00:10:41,902
Yeah. I'll take a layup anytime,
thank you.

197
00:10:41,989 --> 00:10:44,165
Well, you guys are
the professionals here.

198
00:10:44,252 --> 00:10:46,341
But is it usual, there is
a single perpetrator

199
00:10:46,428 --> 00:10:48,604
for this kind
of a jewelry heist?

200
00:10:48,691 --> 00:10:51,085
No, at least two.
Sometimes maybe three or four.

201
00:10:51,172 --> 00:10:53,914
Then there's the timing
of the robbery.

202
00:10:54,001 --> 00:10:55,002
Weren't those diamonds
supposed to be picked up

203
00:10:55,089 --> 00:10:56,177
the next morning?

204
00:10:56,264 --> 00:10:57,613
Yeah, 10:15.
Right when they opened.

205
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:00,007
Gee, you think
maybe it's an inside job?

206
00:11:00,094 --> 00:11:01,443
Uh, yes, I do.

207
00:11:01,530 --> 00:11:03,010
Yeah, well, guess what?
We're way ahead of you.

208
00:11:03,097 --> 00:11:04,141
Because we're doin'
background checks

209
00:11:04,228 --> 00:11:05,490
on the whole
Doverton staff list.

210
00:11:05,577 --> 00:11:09,320
My provisional bet,
provisional, Carl Ward.

211
00:11:09,407 --> 00:11:11,453
Hello. He was shot three times.

212
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:13,542
- Best accomplice is a dead one.
- Well, he's not dead.

213
00:11:13,629 --> 00:11:15,065
Nobody's perfect.

214
00:11:15,152 --> 00:11:18,503
That's it. Carl ward is crooked
because he got shot.

215
00:11:18,590 --> 00:11:20,462
- He's an ex-tennis pro.
- So?

216
00:11:20,549 --> 00:11:23,508
Shady people,
by definition, disappointed.

217
00:11:23,595 --> 00:11:25,380
Should be on center court,
Wimbledon.

218
00:11:25,467 --> 00:11:27,382
Instead he's tossing lobs
to rich old ladies.

219
00:11:27,469 --> 00:11:29,471
- Makes him bitter and twisted.
- Case closed.

220
00:11:29,558 --> 00:11:31,038
I'm just saying.

221
00:11:31,125 --> 00:11:33,693
Go talk to Murphy.
See what he has to say about it.

222
00:11:34,911 --> 00:11:36,696
I'm just sayin'.

223
00:11:51,145 --> 00:11:53,408
Jane knows about us.
I'm sure he does.

224
00:11:56,846 --> 00:11:59,936
Why would it be so bad if people
figured out we're together?

225
00:12:00,023 --> 00:12:04,332
Because...What do you mean, why?

226
00:12:05,289 --> 00:12:08,510
I just mean, is this it?

227
00:12:08,597 --> 00:12:09,511
What? Here? This building?

228
00:12:09,598 --> 00:12:12,035
No, no. I mean us.

229
00:12:12,122 --> 00:12:13,820
Is this all it's gonna be

230
00:12:13,907 --> 00:12:16,431
sneaking around all the time,
afraid someone's gonna find out?

231
00:12:16,518 --> 00:12:20,827
No, it's just the bureau rules.
The rules are the rules, right?

232
00:12:20,914 --> 00:12:22,959
Maybe I'm sick of the rules.

233
00:12:23,046 --> 00:12:24,047
That she blows.

234
00:12:35,145 --> 00:12:36,668
[car door closes]

235
00:12:39,410 --> 00:12:40,585
You don't wanna
to talk about it, huh?

236
00:12:40,672 --> 00:12:44,154
No, I do, just not here,
not on the job.

237
00:12:44,241 --> 00:12:45,416
We'll have to talk
about it sometime.

238
00:12:45,503 --> 00:12:47,331
Yeah. Soon.

239
00:12:49,290 --> 00:12:50,900
CBI.

240
00:12:50,987 --> 00:12:52,554
I'd like to know about
the, uh, resident

241
00:12:52,641 --> 00:12:54,338
who owns the blue beater there.

242
00:12:54,425 --> 00:12:59,169
Murphy? Pays his rent,
makes no trouble.

243
00:12:59,256 --> 00:13:01,084
What'd he do?

244
00:13:01,171 --> 00:13:03,652
Has he had any visitors
recently?

245
00:13:03,739 --> 00:13:07,047
No. Oh, wait.
Yeah, couple of hours ago.

246
00:13:07,134 --> 00:13:08,788
Big guy in a cowboy hat.

247
00:13:08,875 --> 00:13:10,180
Pimped out truck.

248
00:13:10,267 --> 00:13:13,227
Stayed...five minutes,
split.

249
00:13:13,314 --> 00:13:16,665
Does murphy's room have any
rear exit of any kind? A window?

250
00:13:16,752 --> 00:13:18,449
Nope.

251
00:13:18,536 --> 00:13:21,017
What did he do?

252
00:13:21,104 --> 00:13:22,453
Mr. Murphy?

253
00:13:22,540 --> 00:13:25,543
State agents.
We need to talk to you.

254
00:13:27,502 --> 00:13:29,504
Mr. Murphy?

255
00:13:32,159 --> 00:13:33,595
[door creaks]

256
00:13:33,682 --> 00:13:35,640
Mr. Murphy?

257
00:13:37,729 --> 00:13:39,862
Go.

258
00:13:39,949 --> 00:13:40,863
Clear.

259
00:13:49,741 --> 00:13:52,440
Guess that lets Carl Ward
off the hook then, huh?

260
00:14:08,021 --> 00:14:10,327
Ballistics owes me,
expedited the report.

261
00:14:10,414 --> 00:14:13,504
This gun was used to shoot
both Doyle Murphy and Carl Ward.

262
00:14:13,591 --> 00:14:14,941
There were no jewels
in the room or the car.

263
00:14:15,028 --> 00:14:16,899
No prints on the gun.

264
00:14:16,986 --> 00:14:19,771
Start going through all
of Murphy's known associates.

265
00:14:19,859 --> 00:14:21,251
We're looking for a cowboy
in a fancy truck.

266
00:14:21,338 --> 00:14:22,862
Kind of a thin trail.

267
00:14:22,949 --> 00:14:25,603
Murphy's a career criminal.
He has a lot of associates.

268
00:14:25,690 --> 00:14:27,475
Hmm.

269
00:14:27,562 --> 00:14:30,608
- Maybe I can help.
- Just maybe?

270
00:14:30,695 --> 00:14:33,002
Feeling unsure about yourself,
are you?

271
00:14:33,089 --> 00:14:35,048
Don't let one wrong hunch
throw you.

272
00:14:35,135 --> 00:14:38,051
Well, A, we don't know yet
that I'm wrong about Ward.

273
00:14:38,138 --> 00:14:41,271
And B, bring
the motel desk clerk in.

274
00:14:41,358 --> 00:14:43,534
Maybe he can recall a little
more detail than he thinks

275
00:14:43,621 --> 00:14:44,709
about this cowboy.

276
00:14:47,364 --> 00:14:48,800
[Patrick]
'Can you see him?'

277
00:14:48,888 --> 00:14:50,454
Yes.

278
00:14:50,541 --> 00:14:51,978
'What's he doing?'

279
00:14:52,065 --> 00:14:54,894
He's getting out
of the truck.

280
00:14:54,981 --> 00:14:59,768
And he's walking across
the forecourt to Murphy's room.

281
00:14:59,855 --> 00:15:03,163
Can you read the license plate
on the truck?

282
00:15:03,250 --> 00:15:04,207
No.

283
00:15:04,294 --> 00:15:05,295
Look closer.

284
00:15:07,341 --> 00:15:09,082
No, I can't. It's blocked.

285
00:15:09,169 --> 00:15:10,605
What's he wearing?

286
00:15:10,692 --> 00:15:12,389
Cowboy hat, brown.

287
00:15:14,522 --> 00:15:16,132
Black jeans.

288
00:15:16,219 --> 00:15:18,918
Sunglasses, Aviator style.

289
00:15:19,005 --> 00:15:21,921
That's very good, Marty.

290
00:15:22,008 --> 00:15:25,098
What else can you see?

291
00:15:25,185 --> 00:15:27,970
He's wearing a t-shirt
with a Bulldog on the front.

292
00:15:28,057 --> 00:15:30,625
'under it, true blue.'

293
00:15:30,712 --> 00:15:32,801
[indistinct conversations]

294
00:15:39,677 --> 00:15:41,157
[instrumental music]

295
00:15:46,249 --> 00:15:50,123
[dog barks]

296
00:15:55,693 --> 00:15:58,261
Donald Culpepper?

297
00:15:58,348 --> 00:15:59,697
Who the hell are you?

298
00:16:01,656 --> 00:16:02,831
Tsst. Quiet.

299
00:16:05,442 --> 00:16:07,227
I have reason to believe that
you visited with Doyle Murphy

300
00:16:07,314 --> 00:16:10,273
at the Starlite motor court.
Is that correct?

301
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:11,927
That's partially correct.

302
00:16:12,014 --> 00:16:14,451
I went to see him.
He wasn't there, so I left.

303
00:16:14,538 --> 00:16:16,627
Why'd you go see him?

304
00:16:16,714 --> 00:16:20,022
Social visit. Catchin' up.
He was married to my sister.

305
00:16:20,109 --> 00:16:23,373
We have sad news.
Your ex-brother-in-law is dead.

306
00:16:23,460 --> 00:16:24,896
I'm in shock.

307
00:16:26,463 --> 00:16:27,987
How'd that happen?

308
00:16:28,074 --> 00:16:31,512
He was shot. Any idea
who might want to do that?

309
00:16:31,599 --> 00:16:32,556
No.

310
00:16:32,643 --> 00:16:33,993
How about you?

311
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:36,560
I have nothing but love
for Murph.

312
00:16:36,647 --> 00:16:37,953
Why would you think
otherwise?

313
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:39,781
Well, because we read
your rap sheet.

314
00:16:39,868 --> 00:16:42,610
You're a professional criminal
implicated in several murders.

315
00:16:42,697 --> 00:16:46,092
I'm a dynamic businessman
who pushes the envelope.

316
00:16:46,179 --> 00:16:49,747
Anybody can imply anything. It's
another thing to make it stick.

317
00:16:49,834 --> 00:16:51,749
Yeah. Get out.

318
00:16:51,836 --> 00:16:54,361
We need to continue
this conversation downtown.

319
00:17:00,367 --> 00:17:01,498
Thanks.

320
00:17:03,283 --> 00:17:05,763
I deduce from the irritated tone
of your phone conversation

321
00:17:05,850 --> 00:17:09,071
that I was right
about Carl Ward.

322
00:17:09,158 --> 00:17:10,464
Half right.

323
00:17:10,551 --> 00:17:11,726
Go on.

324
00:17:13,771 --> 00:17:15,643
His real name's
Carl Wysowcki.

325
00:17:15,730 --> 00:17:17,819
He was in jail
for kiting checks.

326
00:17:17,906 --> 00:17:20,735
He's got an ex-wife
in Bayonne, New Jersey.

327
00:17:20,822 --> 00:17:24,565
She's owed $90,000
in back alimony.

328
00:17:24,652 --> 00:17:25,783
Half?

329
00:17:36,229 --> 00:17:39,232
So he changed his name.
What of it?

330
00:17:39,319 --> 00:17:41,016
Did you know about
the jail time?

331
00:17:41,103 --> 00:17:42,583
Yes, of course I did.

332
00:17:42,670 --> 00:17:44,237
And the ex-wife?

333
00:17:44,324 --> 00:17:46,065
Of course.
He told me everything.

334
00:17:46,152 --> 00:17:48,371
Hmm, liar.

335
00:17:48,458 --> 00:17:50,460
- Bad liar.
- How dare you.

336
00:17:50,547 --> 00:17:53,115
Well, it's a compliment
to your good character.

337
00:17:53,202 --> 00:17:56,466
You'd rather be a good liar?
Hmm?

338
00:17:58,251 --> 00:18:00,992
I knew everything
about Carl's past.

339
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:02,733
We're glad he told you, ma'am

340
00:18:02,820 --> 00:18:04,909
because we have to consider
the possibility that he was

341
00:18:04,996 --> 00:18:08,435
somehow involved in the jewel
heist and double-crossed.

342
00:18:08,522 --> 00:18:11,655
His honesty with you makes him
far less of a suspect.

343
00:18:11,742 --> 00:18:14,876
Do you have anything further?
My husband needs me.

344
00:18:14,963 --> 00:18:16,443
No, ma'am. Thank you.

345
00:18:18,140 --> 00:18:22,188
- How's he doing?
- He'll live.

346
00:18:22,275 --> 00:18:24,103
They were going to bring him
out of sedation tomorrow.

347
00:18:24,755 --> 00:18:26,453
Excellent.

348
00:18:44,514 --> 00:18:46,168
[Cho]
'Why did you visit with
Doyle Murphy?'

349
00:18:46,255 --> 00:18:48,127
I didn't. He wasn't home.

350
00:18:49,824 --> 00:18:53,262
If he had been home,
what would you have said to him?

351
00:18:53,349 --> 00:18:54,959
Gimme my money.

352
00:18:55,046 --> 00:18:59,312
- He owed you. How much?
- 200 grand and change.

353
00:18:59,399 --> 00:19:01,705
He comes out of incarceration
this last bit he did

354
00:19:01,792 --> 00:19:03,490
and needed to go into rehab.

355
00:19:03,577 --> 00:19:06,928
Junkie son of a gun was there
over a month on my dime.

356
00:19:07,015 --> 00:19:08,408
That's very generous of you.

357
00:19:08,495 --> 00:19:11,324
Nah. What am I gonna do?

358
00:19:11,411 --> 00:19:12,716
At the time,
this was two years back

359
00:19:12,803 --> 00:19:15,502
he's married to my sister.
Moron.

360
00:19:15,589 --> 00:19:16,894
Then he kicks your sister
to the curb

361
00:19:16,981 --> 00:19:18,940
after some
domestic violence problems.

362
00:19:19,027 --> 00:19:21,072
And he still owes you 200 grand.

363
00:19:21,160 --> 00:19:24,032
One, my sister
is a goblin shark.

364
00:19:24,119 --> 00:19:26,295
Freakin' Gandhi would take
a pop at her.

365
00:19:26,382 --> 00:19:28,384
I got no beef with Doyle
over that, believe me.

366
00:19:28,471 --> 00:19:31,257
And two, this money
is what I'm telling ya.

367
00:19:31,344 --> 00:19:33,476
Why would I kill a guy who
owes me that much money?

368
00:19:33,563 --> 00:19:35,043
Because he just made
a very big score.

369
00:19:35,130 --> 00:19:37,350
- Is that right?
- Yes, it is.

370
00:19:37,437 --> 00:19:38,786
Maybe you decided to take
what was owed to you

371
00:19:38,873 --> 00:19:40,266
and a little bit more.

372
00:19:40,353 --> 00:19:41,919
What score is this?
Doverton's?

373
00:19:42,006 --> 00:19:45,967
- You heard about that.
- Was on the TV. Nice work.

374
00:19:46,054 --> 00:19:49,057
Doyle did that? Wow.

375
00:19:49,144 --> 00:19:50,885
Did you ever own a Beretta 950?

376
00:19:50,972 --> 00:19:53,496
As a matter of fact, I did
use to have one of those.

377
00:19:53,583 --> 00:19:55,194
I didn't like the balance.

378
00:19:55,281 --> 00:19:58,762
I sold it to Doyle, why?

379
00:19:58,849 --> 00:20:01,243
Hmm.

380
00:20:01,330 --> 00:20:04,725
Well, I don't care what you're
gonna say. I like him for it.

381
00:20:04,812 --> 00:20:06,857
What do you mean, you don't
care what I have to say?

382
00:20:06,944 --> 00:20:09,382
He's obvious, so you're gonna
say he's not the guy

383
00:20:09,469 --> 00:20:10,948
because, that would be boring.

384
00:20:11,035 --> 00:20:13,777
No, he looks good for it.
Superficially, at least.

385
00:20:13,864 --> 00:20:16,302
I was merely gonna state
that we need to find a link

386
00:20:16,389 --> 00:20:19,392
between him and Doyle
and someone inside Doverton's.

387
00:20:19,479 --> 00:20:21,307
I thought Carl Ward
was your man on the inside.

388
00:20:21,394 --> 00:20:24,788
No. I-I noted
that he was a tennis pro

389
00:20:24,875 --> 00:20:26,225
and extrapolated
from there.

390
00:20:26,312 --> 00:20:28,314
No. No, you said
he was the guy.

391
00:20:28,401 --> 00:20:29,489
Well, if you recall--

392
00:20:29,576 --> 00:20:31,142
Guys. I mean, Boss.

393
00:20:31,230 --> 00:20:33,754
I think I found the link
you're talking about.

394
00:20:33,841 --> 00:20:35,756
The old footage from
the store's security cameras

395
00:20:35,843 --> 00:20:37,323
is saved on a hard drive
off-site.

396
00:20:37,410 --> 00:20:39,499
I figured if Murphy
had been casing the store

397
00:20:39,586 --> 00:20:41,457
I could find him
on the old footage.

398
00:20:44,068 --> 00:20:46,593
- Who's that?
- I'll enhance it.

399
00:20:46,680 --> 00:20:48,899
[keys click]

400
00:20:48,986 --> 00:20:50,597
[Patrick]
'Hmm. Wow.'

401
00:20:50,684 --> 00:20:53,034
Tom Doverton.

402
00:20:57,256 --> 00:20:59,606
It's a customer, I guess.

403
00:20:59,693 --> 00:21:02,173
I'm talking to a customer.

404
00:21:02,261 --> 00:21:03,958
That's the man
who robbed your family store

405
00:21:04,045 --> 00:21:05,438
and shot your uncle.

406
00:21:05,525 --> 00:21:06,830
Are you sure
you don't know him?

407
00:21:06,917 --> 00:21:09,833
Because you're talking
like you're old friends.

408
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:15,535
No. I'm just helpin' a customer
with their purchase. That's all.

409
00:21:15,622 --> 00:21:17,537
And you've never seen
that man before?

410
00:21:17,624 --> 00:21:19,016
No.

411
00:21:19,103 --> 00:21:23,325
And you don't know
the name Doyle Murphy?

412
00:21:23,412 --> 00:21:24,544
No.

413
00:21:26,415 --> 00:21:28,243
The kid's aunt and brother
are here with a lawyer.

414
00:21:28,330 --> 00:21:30,593
Darn. We haven't gotten
anywhere with him yet.

415
00:21:30,680 --> 00:21:32,595
You want me to stall 'em?

416
00:21:32,682 --> 00:21:35,816
It's best just to cut him loose.
He's denying everything anyway.

417
00:21:35,903 --> 00:21:37,470
Unless we can make
some connection

418
00:21:37,557 --> 00:21:39,602
to Culpepper and Doyle, we've
got nothing to hold him on.

419
00:21:39,689 --> 00:21:41,517
Okay.

420
00:21:41,604 --> 00:21:44,564
You go and ask Culpepper about
Tom, I'll deal with the family.

421
00:21:55,270 --> 00:21:56,793
You can take Tom home now.

422
00:21:56,880 --> 00:21:59,318
We just had a couple of
questions, we needed to ask him.

423
00:21:59,405 --> 00:22:00,797
What questions?

424
00:22:00,884 --> 00:22:04,192
I'm sure, he'll fill
you in on the details.

425
00:22:04,279 --> 00:22:06,194
Well, I'd appreciate it
if next time you wish

426
00:22:06,281 --> 00:22:10,677
to speak to a member of my
family, you call me first.

427
00:22:10,764 --> 00:22:13,506
I'm sorry, ma'am.
That's not our practice.

428
00:22:15,508 --> 00:22:17,423
Can I walk you
to the elevator?

429
00:22:17,510 --> 00:22:19,903
[instrumental music]

430
00:22:22,776 --> 00:22:24,995
You do know why I'm letting
you go, don't you?

431
00:22:25,082 --> 00:22:28,042
Let me guess.
I'm innocent.

432
00:22:28,129 --> 00:22:29,739
[Patrick chuckles]

433
00:22:29,826 --> 00:22:32,829
No, I figured
you're stupid.

434
00:22:32,916 --> 00:22:34,875
- Is that so?
- Yeah.

435
00:22:34,962 --> 00:22:36,659
Either you really didn't know
that Doyle was gonna

436
00:22:36,746 --> 00:22:38,661
actually rob
the jewelry store..

437
00:22:38,748 --> 00:22:41,011
...stupid innocent.
Or you did know

438
00:22:41,098 --> 00:22:42,839
and you're gonna lead us
to a clue.

439
00:22:42,926 --> 00:22:44,101
Stupid guilty.

440
00:22:46,060 --> 00:22:48,410
You should be more careful
how you talk to people.

441
00:22:48,497 --> 00:22:51,195
Yeah, figure I'm
pretty safe here.

442
00:22:51,282 --> 00:22:52,458
I mean, what are you
gonna do?

443
00:22:53,981 --> 00:22:55,591
You think I care
where we are?

444
00:22:58,638 --> 00:23:00,030
Go ahead.

445
00:23:00,117 --> 00:23:02,903
Do something, you miserable
little sadist.

446
00:23:02,990 --> 00:23:05,079
Hello, Lisbon. Hi, Dovertons.

447
00:23:05,166 --> 00:23:06,385
What's going on, Jane?

448
00:23:06,472 --> 00:23:08,169
I'm just letting
Mr. Culpepper go.

449
00:23:08,256 --> 00:23:09,866
We don't need him
for anything, do we?

450
00:23:11,041 --> 00:23:12,739
- Do we?
- Sometime.

451
00:23:12,826 --> 00:23:15,089
When you least expect it..

452
00:23:15,176 --> 00:23:16,917
...I'll find you.

453
00:23:20,529 --> 00:23:22,488
- You, uh..
- Wait for the next one.

454
00:23:22,575 --> 00:23:24,533
Alright.

455
00:23:24,620 --> 00:23:27,188
So long, partner.
Happy trails.

456
00:23:29,799 --> 00:23:31,801
Okay, what the hell was that?

457
00:23:31,888 --> 00:23:33,934
- What was what?
- Letting Donny Culpepper go.

458
00:23:34,021 --> 00:23:36,980
You have no right, whatsoever,
to make those kind of decisions.

459
00:23:37,067 --> 00:23:38,634
Well, you weren't
gonna keep him here.

460
00:23:38,721 --> 00:23:42,029
On what? For what?
He wasn't gonna talk. Be honest.

461
00:23:42,116 --> 00:23:43,683
I could have held him
on the weapons charge.

462
00:23:43,770 --> 00:23:45,511
- Oh, please!
- Stop.

463
00:23:45,598 --> 00:23:48,035
There's principle involved.

464
00:23:48,122 --> 00:23:50,167
Don't think I don't know
what you're doing.

465
00:23:50,254 --> 00:23:52,039
What're you talking about?

466
00:23:52,126 --> 00:23:54,258
Showing the Dovertons to Donny
Culpepper. You wanted to see

467
00:23:54,345 --> 00:23:56,086
if he recognized
one of them, didn't you?

468
00:23:56,173 --> 00:23:58,088
You watch too much TV. I just
thought there was no reason

469
00:23:58,175 --> 00:23:59,829
to keep Donny Culpepper here.

470
00:23:59,916 --> 00:24:01,483
Now that's just annoying.
You're not even putting

471
00:24:01,570 --> 00:24:03,398
any effort into the lie.

472
00:24:03,485 --> 00:24:06,445
Hey, boss. Coroner's here.
She got something to show us.

473
00:24:08,403 --> 00:24:09,578
[instrumental music]

474
00:24:13,060 --> 00:24:17,194
Well...it's really beautiful.
Mm?

475
00:24:17,281 --> 00:24:20,502
I found it in Doyle Murphy's
lower intestine.

476
00:24:20,589 --> 00:24:22,809
My supposition is that he
swallowed it

477
00:24:22,896 --> 00:24:24,550
in an attempt to get a larger
share of the loot

478
00:24:24,637 --> 00:24:26,639
before it was divided
with any partners.

479
00:24:26,726 --> 00:24:29,337
Yeah, I guess Murphy
misread that situation, huh?

480
00:24:29,424 --> 00:24:30,904
Did you find anything else
surprising in there?

481
00:24:30,991 --> 00:24:32,775
- Other than that?
- Just that.

482
00:24:32,862 --> 00:24:34,037
[colleagues shrieking]

483
00:24:35,169 --> 00:24:36,562
What the hell was that?

484
00:24:37,214 --> 00:24:38,781
Uh, I..

485
00:24:38,868 --> 00:24:40,653
[Jane clears throat]

486
00:24:40,740 --> 00:24:44,004
...had a hunch
that it was a fake.

487
00:24:44,091 --> 00:24:46,702
- Guess I'm wrong about that.
- Uh, yeah.

488
00:24:47,790 --> 00:24:49,009
Where is it?

489
00:24:49,096 --> 00:24:50,837
Well, the good news is
it's not out there.

490
00:24:50,924 --> 00:24:54,014
So it's in here somewhere.
I'll find it.

491
00:24:55,363 --> 00:24:57,583
Anyone see a little, uh..

492
00:24:57,670 --> 00:24:58,888
[imitates ricocheting sound]

493
00:25:02,675 --> 00:25:03,589
Nope?

494
00:25:07,941 --> 00:25:09,290
There's gotta be a connection
between Doyle Murphy

495
00:25:09,377 --> 00:25:12,032
and Tom Doverton.
Yeah, but w-what was it?

496
00:25:12,119 --> 00:25:13,294
What would bring
those two together?

497
00:25:13,381 --> 00:25:14,643
They have nothing in common.

498
00:25:14,730 --> 00:25:16,340
- What are you guys looking for?
- A diamond.

499
00:25:16,427 --> 00:25:17,385
What did he do?

500
00:25:17,472 --> 00:25:19,126
Hit it with a fire extinguisher.

501
00:25:19,213 --> 00:25:20,693
- Of course.
- I got it!

502
00:25:20,780 --> 00:25:22,912
Oh, thank God.
Where was it?

503
00:25:22,999 --> 00:25:25,045
No, I got
where Doyle and Tom connect.

504
00:25:25,132 --> 00:25:27,221
- Doyle was in rehab.
- Yes.

505
00:25:27,308 --> 00:25:29,789
And Tom Doverton does
look the rehab type.

506
00:25:29,876 --> 00:25:33,619
Van Pelt, find out where Doyle
went to rehab. Keep looking.

507
00:25:33,706 --> 00:25:36,056
Don't worry.
This is justice department.

508
00:25:36,143 --> 00:25:39,059
- Who's gonna steal it here?
- You be quiet!

509
00:25:42,932 --> 00:25:45,326
[muttering indistinctly]

510
00:25:47,633 --> 00:25:50,766
Windward Clarity has the highest
ratio of staff-to-patient

511
00:25:50,853 --> 00:25:53,029
of any facility
in the state.

512
00:25:53,116 --> 00:25:55,641
Our philosophy mixes
traditional western practices

513
00:25:55,728 --> 00:25:58,731
with spiritual aspects
of the East.

514
00:25:58,818 --> 00:26:01,777
Now, may I ask which one of you
needs our help

515
00:26:01,864 --> 00:26:03,344
or is it both of you?

516
00:26:03,431 --> 00:26:05,215
- Actually, we just wanted--
- It's her.

517
00:26:05,302 --> 00:26:07,391
- No.
- Oh, darling, please.

518
00:26:07,478 --> 00:26:10,438
Can we, just this once, do it
my way? Just once.

519
00:26:11,134 --> 00:26:12,832
Thank you.

520
00:26:12,919 --> 00:26:15,704
In the beginning,
it was just social but..

521
00:26:15,791 --> 00:26:18,925
...well, more recently..

522
00:26:19,012 --> 00:26:21,275
...it's been starting
to worry both of us.

523
00:26:21,362 --> 00:26:22,537
Right, darling?

524
00:26:24,713 --> 00:26:26,497
Right this way.
I'll introduce you to one of our

525
00:26:26,585 --> 00:26:27,760
intake specialists.

526
00:26:27,847 --> 00:26:29,500
Fantastic.

527
00:26:29,588 --> 00:26:32,939
Oh, uh, that's the office.
I'm gonna have to take this.

528
00:26:33,026 --> 00:26:34,897
Uh, baby, you go ahead,
take a look.

529
00:26:34,984 --> 00:26:37,030
I'll be five minutes.
I'll be right there.

530
00:26:37,117 --> 00:26:38,248
Look after her.

531
00:26:39,336 --> 00:26:40,860
I'm very proud of you.

532
00:26:40,947 --> 00:26:43,297
- I understand your nervousness.
- I love you.

533
00:26:43,384 --> 00:26:45,168
[Dr. Joseph]
'It's completely natural.'

534
00:26:45,255 --> 00:26:47,606
'But you've already taken
the first step in recognizing..'

535
00:26:55,178 --> 00:26:56,353
[telephone ringing]

536
00:26:58,007 --> 00:26:59,574
[instrumental music]

537
00:27:38,482 --> 00:27:41,442
Our kitchen is capable
of preparing food for any diet.

538
00:27:41,529 --> 00:27:46,403
And of course, we have a pair
of on-site nutritionists.

539
00:27:46,490 --> 00:27:49,537
Um, your husband is
taking quite a long time.

540
00:27:49,624 --> 00:27:52,453
You should understand...
he's not really my husband.

541
00:27:52,540 --> 00:27:53,802
Oh, that's quite alright.

542
00:27:53,889 --> 00:27:55,412
We're not judgmental about such
things here.

543
00:27:56,675 --> 00:27:58,241
That's not what I mean.

544
00:28:01,680 --> 00:28:03,594
Whoa.

545
00:28:03,682 --> 00:28:05,771
Uppers and Downers.
Uppers and..

546
00:28:05,858 --> 00:28:07,990
...you're so rock 'n' roll,
doctor.

547
00:28:08,077 --> 00:28:09,644
That is private material.

548
00:28:09,731 --> 00:28:11,646
Doctor-patient privilege
is sacrosanct.

549
00:28:11,733 --> 00:28:13,996
Yes, I know.
That's why we did it this way.

550
00:28:14,083 --> 00:28:15,389
This is a murder investigation.

551
00:28:15,476 --> 00:28:17,608
We can be as hard-ass
as we want. I found it..

552
00:28:17,696 --> 00:28:19,567
...the, uh, patient roster
for the first two weeks

553
00:28:19,654 --> 00:28:21,177
of Doyle Murphy's stay.

554
00:28:21,264 --> 00:28:24,659
Guess who his acting sponsor was
when he first arrived?

555
00:28:24,746 --> 00:28:26,661
- Tom Doverton?
- No!

556
00:28:26,748 --> 00:28:28,968
- George.
- The brother?

557
00:28:29,055 --> 00:28:30,273
Yeah.

558
00:28:30,360 --> 00:28:31,535
I will be calling
your superiors.

559
00:28:31,622 --> 00:28:32,754
You know, a lot of people say
that to me

560
00:28:32,841 --> 00:28:34,016
and you know what I say?

561
00:28:34,103 --> 00:28:36,323
- I say--
- Let's go. Come on.

562
00:28:37,498 --> 00:28:40,806
[rattling pills]

563
00:28:40,893 --> 00:28:42,721
Get some help, doc.
That stuff will kill you.

564
00:28:45,201 --> 00:28:46,855
[instrumental music]

565
00:28:48,291 --> 00:28:49,423
[door creaks]

566
00:28:52,208 --> 00:28:53,993
George isn't here.

567
00:28:54,080 --> 00:28:57,997
He's already left.
He's not guilty, you know.

568
00:28:58,084 --> 00:28:59,476
How'd you know we were coming?

569
00:28:59,563 --> 00:29:01,522
The director
of the clinic called.

570
00:29:01,609 --> 00:29:04,394
He told us what you'd done.

571
00:29:04,481 --> 00:29:07,180
George was in rehab
with Doyle Murphy, that's true.

572
00:29:07,267 --> 00:29:08,834
But that's all that it was.

573
00:29:08,921 --> 00:29:11,314
So you're saying if we dig,
we won't find any more contact

574
00:29:11,401 --> 00:29:12,620
between George and Doyle?

575
00:29:12,707 --> 00:29:14,840
He came into the store.

576
00:29:14,927 --> 00:29:17,407
A guy like that
at Doverton & Company, I mean..

577
00:29:17,494 --> 00:29:19,975
Come on.
If my aunt found out..

578
00:29:20,062 --> 00:29:21,629
We need to talk to George.

579
00:29:21,716 --> 00:29:23,631
- He's gone.
- Oh, really?

580
00:29:25,502 --> 00:29:28,375
Uh, I think he's hiding
behind the door.

581
00:29:28,462 --> 00:29:29,637
George?

582
00:29:32,379 --> 00:29:34,337
George, come out.

583
00:29:45,305 --> 00:29:47,133
I'm sorry, Tom.

584
00:29:47,220 --> 00:29:48,395
You were just trying
to help.

585
00:29:49,700 --> 00:29:51,441
but we both know
there's no..

586
00:29:51,528 --> 00:29:53,095
...hiding from something
like this.

587
00:29:58,187 --> 00:29:59,623
I had nothing to do
with the robbery

588
00:29:59,710 --> 00:30:01,016
or Doyle Murphy's murder.

589
00:30:01,103 --> 00:30:03,976
I only lied to protect
my aunt from..

590
00:30:04,063 --> 00:30:05,804
...the shame of my addiction.

591
00:30:07,718 --> 00:30:10,896
Tom, call Dicky Purcell.
Tell him to..

592
00:30:10,983 --> 00:30:12,680
...meet me
at the CBI offices.

593
00:30:12,767 --> 00:30:14,116
- Yeah.
- After you.

594
00:30:25,388 --> 00:30:27,216
Excuse me.
Do you work here?

595
00:30:27,303 --> 00:30:30,045
Uh, certainly, sir.
Um, how can I help you?

596
00:30:30,132 --> 00:30:32,700
My wife...it's her birthday.

597
00:30:32,787 --> 00:30:35,964
Of course. Of course.
Uh, Michelle?

598
00:30:36,051 --> 00:30:37,226
Michelle here, would be happy
to help you.

599
00:30:37,313 --> 00:30:38,793
Thank you.

600
00:30:38,880 --> 00:30:40,577
- Mr. Doverton?
- Yeah.

601
00:30:40,664 --> 00:30:42,144
I need a signature
for this.

602
00:30:48,498 --> 00:30:49,630
There you go.

603
00:30:51,197 --> 00:30:52,328
What?

604
00:30:56,115 --> 00:30:58,378
"You'll get a call in 2 hours.

605
00:30:58,465 --> 00:31:01,337
"Take the jewels
where you are told.

606
00:31:01,424 --> 00:31:04,210
No cops or your aunt dies."

607
00:31:10,172 --> 00:31:11,521
[instrumental music]

608
00:31:14,960 --> 00:31:16,700
She's gone.

609
00:31:17,832 --> 00:31:18,920
Oh, God.

610
00:31:19,007 --> 00:31:21,575
Why would anybody kidnap
Aunt Esther?

611
00:31:21,662 --> 00:31:24,534
Someone thinks your brother was
partners with Doyle Murphy.

612
00:31:24,621 --> 00:31:26,406
Well, you think so.
Why shouldn't everyone else?

613
00:31:26,493 --> 00:31:28,495
Donny Culpepper?

614
00:31:28,582 --> 00:31:29,975
What? Who's Donny Culpepper?

615
00:31:30,062 --> 00:31:31,977
You saw him when we were
waiting for the elevator.

616
00:31:32,064 --> 00:31:33,848
Doyle Murphy owed him
a lot of money.

617
00:31:33,935 --> 00:31:36,633
He might think that that debt
transfers with the diamonds.

618
00:31:36,720 --> 00:31:37,852
Okay, but we don't have
the diamonds.

619
00:31:37,939 --> 00:31:39,158
Well, he doesn't know that.

620
00:31:39,245 --> 00:31:41,116
Well, you have to go
arrest him right away.

621
00:31:41,203 --> 00:31:43,989
That's what we're trying to do,
Mr. Doverton.

622
00:31:44,076 --> 00:31:46,600
Agent Van Pelt is working with
local police as we speak.

623
00:31:46,687 --> 00:31:50,604
- We're pursuing several leads.
- But the note says no police.

624
00:31:50,691 --> 00:31:52,954
Well, it's a little late
for that.

625
00:31:53,041 --> 00:31:55,609
Whoa. What are you gonna do? How
are you gonna get our aunt back?

626
00:31:55,696 --> 00:31:58,655
We'll do everything we can
to find her.

627
00:31:58,742 --> 00:32:01,310
In the meantime, when the
kidnappers call, you have to try

628
00:32:01,397 --> 00:32:02,921
and stall them.

629
00:32:06,011 --> 00:32:08,274
- Take that.
- Okay.

630
00:32:08,361 --> 00:32:09,579
Okay.

631
00:32:22,462 --> 00:32:25,421
- It's almost time. You ready?
- Yeah, ready.

632
00:32:25,508 --> 00:32:29,425
- And you?
- I don't know.

633
00:32:29,512 --> 00:32:31,036
Just stay calm.

634
00:32:31,123 --> 00:32:33,908
Keep the kidnapper on the line
and keep him calm.

635
00:32:33,995 --> 00:32:35,649
Remember, keep going
as long as you can, okay?

636
00:32:35,736 --> 00:32:36,955
[phone vibrating]

637
00:32:38,782 --> 00:32:40,654
- Headphones?
- Yeah, in the other room.

638
00:32:42,830 --> 00:32:44,005
Ready?

639
00:32:45,006 --> 00:32:46,181
Here we go.

640
00:32:52,492 --> 00:32:54,102
Hello?

641
00:32:54,189 --> 00:32:57,062
[distorted voice]
'Do you have the jewels ready?'

642
00:32:57,149 --> 00:32:58,324
[no audio]

643
00:33:00,021 --> 00:33:02,023
How do I know
you have my aunt?

644
00:33:02,110 --> 00:33:04,243
- I-I demand to--
- No.

645
00:33:04,330 --> 00:33:07,637
[kidnapper]
'You don't demand anything.
You shut up and you listen.'

646
00:33:07,724 --> 00:33:10,075
I-I-I'm sorry.
I'm sorry.

647
00:33:10,162 --> 00:33:12,903
- I-I just need proof before--
- Proof?

648
00:33:12,991 --> 00:33:14,514
[kidnapper]
'I'll give you proof.'

649
00:33:14,601 --> 00:33:16,690
[Esther]
'Hello? George?'

650
00:33:16,777 --> 00:33:18,866
Esther, are you alright?

651
00:33:18,953 --> 00:33:21,869
[kidnapper]
'There. That's your proof.'

652
00:33:21,956 --> 00:33:23,914
'If you want to see her
alive again'

653
00:33:24,002 --> 00:33:26,700
'you're gonna bring me
the diamonds.'

654
00:33:26,787 --> 00:33:29,224
I-I-I...I can't do that.

655
00:33:29,311 --> 00:33:31,096
- I-I...
- Got him.

656
00:33:31,183 --> 00:33:32,619
[kidnapper]
'You think this is
some kind of a game?'

657
00:33:32,706 --> 00:33:35,013
No! No. I-I-I'm sorry.

658
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:36,927
[kidnapper]
'Are you gonna give me
the jewels?'

659
00:33:37,015 --> 00:33:41,019
I-I...I don't have the jewels.
I'm trying to tell you.

660
00:33:41,106 --> 00:33:44,544
[kidnapper]
'Alright, then.
This is your decision.'

661
00:33:44,631 --> 00:33:45,980
[gunshot]

662
00:33:46,067 --> 00:33:48,026
Aunt Esther!

663
00:33:48,113 --> 00:33:53,074
[kidnapper]
'You're next.
Now, I'm coming for you.'

664
00:33:53,161 --> 00:33:54,641
Oh, my God.

665
00:33:54,728 --> 00:33:55,946
[groans]

666
00:33:58,819 --> 00:34:00,125
Stay right there.

667
00:34:02,475 --> 00:34:03,693
Rigsby!

668
00:34:12,833 --> 00:34:14,139
[vibrates and rings]

669
00:34:15,575 --> 00:34:16,750
[ringing continues]

670
00:34:25,846 --> 00:34:27,239
Hello?

671
00:34:27,326 --> 00:34:29,502
[kidnapper]
'Where are the jewels, Tom?'

672
00:34:29,589 --> 00:34:33,288
- 'We both know you have 'em.'
- They're going to catch you.

673
00:34:33,375 --> 00:34:35,986
They're going to get you
right now.

674
00:34:36,074 --> 00:34:37,901
[kidnapper]
'No, no, no.'

675
00:34:37,988 --> 00:34:40,643
[kidnapper]
'I'm gonna get you,Tom.'

676
00:34:40,730 --> 00:34:42,993
[kidnapper]
'I know everything
about you.'

677
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:46,214
[kidnapper]
'Yeah, Doyle Murphy
told me everything.'

678
00:34:46,301 --> 00:34:48,303
Screw you.

679
00:34:48,390 --> 00:34:50,914
I'll give you $50,000..

680
00:34:51,001 --> 00:34:53,700
...$50,000 to leave me
and my brother alone.

681
00:34:53,787 --> 00:34:55,963
[kidnapper]
'Oh, you think
you're so tough.'

682
00:34:56,050 --> 00:34:59,662
[kidnapper]
'You give me what I want, or I
will come and take it from you.'

683
00:34:59,749 --> 00:35:03,057
I'll make it $75,000,
but no more.

684
00:35:03,144 --> 00:35:05,277
I'm not givin' up
the diamonds.

685
00:35:05,364 --> 00:35:08,976
You stupid, rich brat.

686
00:35:09,063 --> 00:35:11,848
You think
you can dictate terms?

687
00:35:11,935 --> 00:35:14,068
Not this time, Tom.

688
00:35:14,155 --> 00:35:15,287
[distorted voice]
'Not this time.'

689
00:35:15,374 --> 00:35:18,551
'I'm gonna make you pay
and pay.'

690
00:35:18,638 --> 00:35:20,379
You try it.

691
00:35:20,466 --> 00:35:23,208
You try it,
and you see what happens.

692
00:35:23,295 --> 00:35:25,297
Tough guy, huh?

693
00:35:25,384 --> 00:35:27,864
You're gonna give me the same
thing you gave to Doyle Murphy?

694
00:35:27,951 --> 00:35:30,084
That's right.

695
00:35:30,171 --> 00:35:34,523
You push me hard enough,
and you'll see.

696
00:35:34,610 --> 00:35:38,571
Okay.
Thanks, Tom. That's great.

697
00:35:38,658 --> 00:35:42,270
We got it all on tape. You're
going down. Give me the phone.

698
00:35:42,357 --> 00:35:44,316
Hands behind your back.

699
00:35:44,403 --> 00:35:49,059
You're under arrest
for the murder of Doyle Murphy.

700
00:35:50,670 --> 00:35:51,758
Let's go.

701
00:35:53,890 --> 00:35:55,153
Like a charm.

702
00:35:59,896 --> 00:36:01,594
Ah, tea.

703
00:36:01,681 --> 00:36:02,638
Lovely.

704
00:36:07,165 --> 00:36:10,037
Ms. Doverton?

705
00:36:10,124 --> 00:36:12,822
I'm sorry, but we've had
to mislead you a little

706
00:36:12,909 --> 00:36:16,348
to solve this crime.

707
00:36:16,435 --> 00:36:19,568
I'm afraid I've got
some difficult news.

708
00:36:19,655 --> 00:36:22,658
Your nephew, Tom, was involved
in the robbery.

709
00:36:28,621 --> 00:36:29,796
Tom?

710
00:36:31,145 --> 00:36:32,364
[instrumental music]

711
00:36:46,769 --> 00:36:49,163
[music continues]

712
00:36:59,042 --> 00:37:00,479
[monitor beeping steadily]

713
00:37:00,566 --> 00:37:01,697
Ahem.

714
00:37:03,786 --> 00:37:07,747
Oh, hey, Carl. Welcome back.

715
00:37:07,834 --> 00:37:09,052
[monitor beeping steadily]

716
00:37:09,139 --> 00:37:11,794
- Where am I?
- In the hospital.

717
00:37:11,881 --> 00:37:14,623
You just came out
of an induced coma.

718
00:37:14,710 --> 00:37:16,103
Can I ask you a question?

719
00:37:16,190 --> 00:37:18,061
What happened to me?
Who are you?

720
00:37:18,148 --> 00:37:24,067
Never mind that.
Tell me...Esther Doverton..

721
00:37:24,154 --> 00:37:26,592
...it was all a scam, right?

722
00:37:26,679 --> 00:37:29,638
You just wanted to cash
in with a rich, old broad.

723
00:37:29,725 --> 00:37:32,467
- Excuse me?
- Oh, come on.

724
00:37:32,554 --> 00:37:35,427
You don't need to be coy
with me. It's a great score.

725
00:37:35,514 --> 00:37:38,256
Plus, she's,
uh, she's pretty good-looking..

726
00:37:38,343 --> 00:37:41,520
...which is an added bonus
with these kind of scams.

727
00:37:41,607 --> 00:37:44,262
Are you talking
about my wife?

728
00:37:44,349 --> 00:37:45,741
Who the hell
do you think you are?

729
00:37:45,828 --> 00:37:47,047
Wouldn't that be
a better question

730
00:37:47,134 --> 00:37:52,748
for me to ask you,
Mr. Wysocki?

731
00:37:52,835 --> 00:37:55,708
- So that's it.
- Had to end sometime.

732
00:37:55,795 --> 00:37:57,405
You had a pretty good run.

733
00:37:57,492 --> 00:37:58,972
And you know what?
Between you and me

734
00:37:59,059 --> 00:38:01,017
I think you could squeeze
a little more nickel

735
00:38:01,104 --> 00:38:03,281
out of this poor old bat.
What do you think?

736
00:38:03,368 --> 00:38:05,935
Don't you talk about her
like that.

737
00:38:06,022 --> 00:38:07,328
That woman is
the only good thing

738
00:38:07,415 --> 00:38:08,764
that's ever happened to me.

739
00:38:08,851 --> 00:38:11,898
- I love her.
- You sure about that?

740
00:38:11,985 --> 00:38:14,030
Shut up and get out of here,
you son of a..

741
00:38:14,117 --> 00:38:15,597
Okay, gotcha.

742
00:38:21,603 --> 00:38:22,952
Told you he loved you.

743
00:38:28,741 --> 00:38:30,003
'Stop.'

744
00:38:30,090 --> 00:38:31,918
What are you
trying to do, kill yourself?

745
00:38:32,005 --> 00:38:35,835
I'm so sorry.
I never meant to hurt you.

746
00:38:41,144 --> 00:38:42,363
[instrumental music]

747
00:38:52,112 --> 00:38:53,287
Got it.

748
00:38:59,380 --> 00:39:00,860
Ah.

749
00:39:00,947 --> 00:39:02,949
A long time ago,
that might have been a frog

750
00:39:03,036 --> 00:39:05,473
or a fern or a dinosaur.

751
00:39:05,560 --> 00:39:07,083
Then it died,
turned into coal..

752
00:39:07,170 --> 00:39:09,782
Yeah, I know.
I did first grade geology, too.

753
00:39:09,869 --> 00:39:13,002
Well, I'm just saying, it's, uh,
just compressed carbon to me.

754
00:39:13,089 --> 00:39:15,265
If you want to keep it,
I never saw a thing.

755
00:39:15,353 --> 00:39:19,182
What? Get outta here. I wasn't
even thinkin' about that.

756
00:39:19,269 --> 00:39:21,184
Well, if you say so.

757
00:39:21,271 --> 00:39:24,187
Seriously, you'd let me just
walk out of here with this?

758
00:39:24,274 --> 00:39:25,580
Do you want to?

759
00:39:25,667 --> 00:39:28,235
No, of course not.
I mean, yes, I'm tempted.

760
00:39:28,322 --> 00:39:30,846
Okay, then.
Put it in your pocket.

761
00:39:30,933 --> 00:39:32,326
This is a trap, isn't it?

762
00:39:32,413 --> 00:39:35,590
Are you nuts? What do I care
about your integrity?

763
00:39:35,677 --> 00:39:37,200
Oh, yeah, trap.

764
00:39:38,419 --> 00:39:40,769
- Okay. Huh.
- Huh.

765
00:39:43,337 --> 00:39:46,949
- Oh, uh, Grace.
- Hi.

766
00:39:47,036 --> 00:39:48,951
Rigsby put something
in your pocket.

767
00:39:49,038 --> 00:39:50,213
What?

768
00:39:57,003 --> 00:39:58,178
Wow.

769
00:40:02,487 --> 00:40:05,141
H-he's messing with you.

770
00:40:05,228 --> 00:40:07,579
Oh, it's compressed carbon.

771
00:40:07,666 --> 00:40:09,232
If you want to keep it...

772
00:40:09,319 --> 00:40:12,497
I don't know about Rigsby,
but, uh..

773
00:40:12,584 --> 00:40:14,499
I wouldn't tell a soul.

774
00:40:27,773 --> 00:40:28,469
[male narrator]
'Stay tuned for scenes
from our next episode.'

775
00:40:32,212 --> 00:40:34,344
[theme music]

